---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What is the Wolverhampton PHV test?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "The Wolverhampton PHV test is the knowledge test you must pass to get a private hire vehicle driver's licence (the 'taxi badge') from City of Wolverhampton Council. It is a multiple-choice test covering topics such as safeguarding, plying for hire, disability awareness, customer care, licensing and taxi law.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What score do I need to pass the Wolverhampton PHV test?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "You must score at least 21 out of 28 (75%) AND answer all 8 key questions correctly. Both parts of the rule apply: a high overall score does not save you if you get a key question wrong.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How long is the Wolverhampton PHV test?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "The test has 28 multiple-choice questions and you have 45 minutes to complete it — plenty of time if you know the material and read each question carefully.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How many attempts do I get at the Wolverhampton PHV test?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Only 3 attempts are permitted. If you don't pass within these 3 attempts, your application is voided and you'll need to wait 12 months before starting the application and training process again. Each attempt costs around £50 (training course plus test).",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "How should I prepare for the Wolverhampton PHV test?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Practise under real exam conditions — timed mock tests of 28 questions in 45 minutes, marked with the real pass rules. Drill the key question topics (safeguarding, plying for hire and disability awareness) until your answers are automatic, and review every question you get wrong until you understand why the correct answer is correct.",
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Pass the Wolverhampton PHV Test: 2026 Guide"
  description="Pass the Wolverhampton PHV test first time: our 2026 guide covers applying, the test format, the 8 key questions rule, attempts, costs and how to prepare."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <main class="md:flex md:justify-center">
    <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-[750px]">
      <h1>How to Pass the Wolverhampton PHV Test: 2026 Guide</h1>

      <p>
        This guide explains <strong>how to pass the Wolverhampton PHV
        test</strong> — from applying for your badge to walking out of the
        test centre with a pass. The test itself is straightforward once you
        understand how it works, but it has one rule that catches out
        well-prepared drivers, and a limit on attempts that makes each try
        count. Everything below is in plain English, step by step.
      </p>

      <h2>What is the Wolverhampton PHV test?</h2>

      <p>
        To drive a private hire vehicle licensed by City of Wolverhampton
        Council, you need a PHV driver's licence — often called the "taxi
        badge". Part of getting that licence is passing a knowledge test that
        checks you understand the things a professional driver must know:
        safeguarding passengers, the law on picking up work, the rights of
        disabled passengers, customer care, and the rules of your licence.
      </p>

      <p>
        Many drivers across the UK apply through Wolverhampton because its
        licensing process is streamlined — but the test is mandatory and taken
        seriously, so it deserves proper preparation.
      </p>

      <h2>How do I apply for the Wolverhampton PHV badge?</h2>

      <p>
        Before you can sit the test, you need to meet the basic requirements
        and get your paperwork in order. In summary:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Be at least 21 years old</strong> and have held a full UK
          driving licence for at least 12 months.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pass a medical.</strong> You'll need a Group 2 medical — the
          higher standard that applies to professional drivers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pass an enhanced DBS check</strong> (criminal record check).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Gather your documents</strong> — identity, right to work,
          and licence details — and submit your application to the council.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Book your training and test.</strong> The knowledge test is
          taken alongside a short training course.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Always check the council's official guidance for the current
        requirements and fees before you apply — details can change.
      </p>

      <h2>What's in the test?</h2>

      <p>
        The test is multiple-choice, with <strong>28 questions</strong> and
        <strong>45 minutes</strong> to complete it. To pass, you must meet
        <strong>both</strong> parts of the pass rule:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Score at least <strong>21 out of 28</strong> overall (75%),
          <strong>and</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          Answer <strong>all 8 key questions correctly</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The questions draw on the full syllabus: safeguarding, plying for
        hire, disability awareness, customer care and safety, licensing and
        compliance, vehicle and road safety, and law and regulations.
      </p>

      <h2>What are the 8 key questions?</h2>

      <p>
        Hidden inside those 28 questions are <strong>8 key
        questions</strong> covering safeguarding, plying for hire and
        disability awareness — the areas where a driver's mistake could put a
        passenger at risk. The rule is strict: <strong>get even one key
        question wrong and you fail the whole test</strong>, no matter how
        good your overall score is. You could answer 27 out of 28 correctly
        and still fail.
      </p>

      <p>
        This rule is the single most common reason well-prepared drivers fail,
        so it's worth understanding properly — read our full guide to
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/8-key-questions"
          >the 8 key questions on the Wolverhampton PHV test</a
        >
        for what they cover and how to make sure you never drop one.
      </p>

      <h2>How many attempts do I get?</h2>

      <p>
        Only <strong>3 attempts</strong> are permitted. If you don't pass
        within these 3 attempts, your application is voided and you'll need to
        wait <strong>12 months</strong> before starting the application and
        training process again. Each attempt costs around
        <strong>£50</strong> (training course plus test).
      </p>

      <p>
        That's why the goal isn't just to pass — it's to pass
        <strong>first time</strong>. Between fees, lost earnings and the risk
        of a year-long wait, thorough preparation is by far the cheapest
        option.
      </p>

      <h2>How should I prepare?</h2>

      <p>
        The drivers who pass first time tend to do the same three things:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Practise under real exam conditions.</strong> Take timed
          mock tests — 28 questions in 45 minutes — marked with the real pass
          rules: 21 correct plus all 8 key questions. Mock results only mean
          something if they're scored the way the real test is scored.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Drill the key question topics until they're
          automatic.</strong> Safeguarding, plying for hire and disability
          awareness decide your result. Keep practising them until you stop
          getting any wrong — every time, not just most of the time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Learn from every mistake.</strong> Don't just note that an
          answer was wrong — understand why the correct answer is correct.
          That's the difference between memorising and actually knowing.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        If you want all of that in one place, the
        <a
          href="https://apps.apple.com/gb/app/phv-prep-uk-taxi-badge-test/id6776364932"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">PHV Prep UK app</a
        >
        gives you 300+ exam-style practice questions, timed mock exams scored
        exactly like the real test, weak-area tracking, and a plain-English AI
        tutor for anything you don't understand.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>

      <p>
        Start with the rule that decides most results: our guide to
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/8-key-questions"
          >the 8 key questions on the Wolverhampton PHV test</a
        >. You can also learn more about the app on the
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk">PHV Prep UK page</a>, get help via
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/support">support</a>, or read our
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>PHV Prep UK is an independent study aid.</strong> It is not
        affiliated with, authorised by, or endorsed by City of Wolverhampton
        Council or any licensing authority. Always check the council's
        official guidance for the latest test requirements and fees.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
